Practice 3 grammar (EBNF) for the Practice 2 language, widened by Task 4
to full +, -, * expressions with precedence and left associativity.

Notation: | alternatives, [ x ] optional, { x } zero or more,
"quoted" terminals are token kinds the lexer emits verbatim,
unquoted names are non-terminals with a rule below.

program    ::= { statement } exit_stmt
statement  ::= decl | assign
decl       ::= "i32" [ "mut" ] ident "{" expr "}"
assign     ::= ident ":=" expr
exit_stmt  ::= "exit" factor
expr       ::= term { ( "+" | "-" ) term }
term       ::= factor { "*" factor }
factor     ::= number | ident

Terminals ident and number come from the lexer as "identifier" and
"constant" tokens; how they are spelled is the lexer's rule, not the
grammar's. "i32", "mut", "exit", "{", "}", ":=", "+", "-", "*" are the
lexer's "typename"/"specifier"/"statement" keywords, block start/end and
operator tokens, used here with the text they carry.

One statement per line; blank lines carry no tokens and are skipped
before this grammar ever sees them (a lexer concern, per Practice 2).
exit_stmt is the last line, and there is exactly one — program requires
{ statement } to end with exactly one exit_stmt, not zero, not more,
and nothing after it; the parser enforces this since EBNF has no way to
say "last line".

Semantics the grammar cannot express — a name declared once, before its
first use, and only a mut variable assigned — are not covered by any
rule above; they are checked during the AST walk in gen.py instead. That
* binds tighter than + and - is not semantics: it falls out of the shape
of expr/term/factor and needs no rule of its own.
